WEBVTT

00:00:00.600 --> 00:00:02.300
A chessboard may look like a collection

00:00:02.300 --> 00:00:03.190
of little squares.

00:00:03.840 --> 00:00:06.610
Its colours can reveal something numbers alone cannot:

00:00:07.000 --> 00:00:08.470
whether a covering is impossible.

00:00:09.570 --> 00:00:13.080
In AMC, you may need to spot an odd or even pattern.

00:00:13.710 --> 00:00:16.830
In a Rossmoyne style problem, explain why every allowed

00:00:16.830 --> 00:00:18.580
piece follows the same rule.

00:00:19.290 --> 00:00:21.730
A MAWA investigation might explore which boards

00:00:21.730 --> 00:00:22.640
can be tiled.

00:00:23.210 --> 00:00:24.920
Let us move from pairs to colouring.

00:00:25.730 --> 00:00:27.740
Start with a three by three chessboard.

00:00:28.170 --> 00:00:30.700
The top-left square is blue and the colours alternate.

00:00:31.330 --> 00:00:31.970
Count each colour.

00:00:31.970 --> 00:00:34.580
Is the number of squares odd or even?

00:00:35.500 --> 00:00:37.960
There are five blue squares and four gold

00:00:37.960 --> 00:00:39.630
squares, nine altogether.

00:00:40.300 --> 00:00:42.250
They cannot all be grouped into pairs:

00:00:42.800 --> 00:00:43.790
one square remains.

00:00:45.153 --> 00:00:46.863
Take a full four by four board.

00:00:47.413 --> 00:00:49.933
In the top row, cross out the two squares farthest

00:00:49.933 --> 00:00:50.523
to the left.

00:00:51.133 --> 00:00:52.473
One is blue and one is gold.

00:00:52.473 --> 00:00:54.543
Can dominoes cover the rest?

00:00:55.153 --> 00:00:57.743
Each domino covers two squares sharing an edge.

00:00:58.950 --> 00:00:59.380
Yes.

00:00:59.950 --> 00:01:02.790
Place one domino across the remaining two squares in

00:01:02.790 --> 00:01:03.460
the top row.

00:01:03.930 --> 00:01:06.710
Then place two horizontal dominoes across each of the

00:01:06.710 --> 00:01:07.700
three lower rows.

00:01:08.290 --> 00:01:11.300
That is seven dominoes, covering fourteen squares.

00:01:12.390 --> 00:01:13.720
Here is a harder puzzle.

00:01:14.250 --> 00:01:16.490
A four by four board has its top-left and

00:01:16.490 --> 00:01:18.000
bottom-right corners removed.

00:01:18.790 --> 00:01:21.380
Each domino covers two squares sharing an edge.

00:01:22.070 --> 00:01:24.510
The challenge is to cover the remaining board with seven

00:01:24.510 --> 00:01:26.160
dominoes and no overlap.

00:01:26.570 --> 00:01:27.140
Is that possible?

00:01:31.080 --> 00:01:33.230
Colour the full board like a chessboard.

00:01:33.600 --> 00:01:35.950
There are eight blue squares and eight gold squares.

00:01:36.480 --> 00:01:38.490
Each domino, horizontal or vertical,

00:01:38.940 --> 00:01:40.070
covers one of each colour.

00:01:41.010 --> 00:01:43.100
The two removed corners are both blue.

00:01:43.610 --> 00:01:45.880
That leaves six blue and eight gold squares.

00:01:46.430 --> 00:01:49.060
Seven dominoes would need seven of each colour.

00:01:50.030 --> 00:01:52.000
Since only six blue squares remain,

00:01:52.430 --> 00:01:53.620
the covering is impossible.

00:01:54.770 --> 00:01:57.060
It is a mistake to stop at fourteen squares.

00:01:57.690 --> 00:02:00.090
Fourteen is divisible by two, but the colours

00:02:00.090 --> 00:02:01.000
do not balance.

00:02:01.590 --> 00:02:04.380
And colour balance alone does not guarantee a covering:

00:02:04.850 --> 00:02:06.680
the squares must still join up legally.

00:02:07.730 --> 00:02:11.100
In an exam, identify what every allowed piece does.

00:02:11.610 --> 00:02:13.340
Pick a colouring that reveals the pattern.

00:02:13.870 --> 00:02:15.960
Count the colours and write a clear reason,

00:02:16.110 --> 00:02:17.060
not just an answer.
